WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.730 --> 00:00:11.830
If I were to ask you ‘what is the scariest
type of science fiction?’ you probably wouldn’t

00:00:11.830 --> 00:00:16.720
say ‘Evolutionary Horror.’ …Mainly because
it’s a name I came up with for this video,

00:00:16.720 --> 00:00:23.350
but also because it doesn’t sound particularly
frightening. Evolution isn’t scary… or

00:00:23.350 --> 00:00:29.289
is it? Twisting through the history of sci-fi
is a hidden subgenre that derives terror from

00:00:29.289 --> 00:00:34.570
the altered self — from the uncanny dread
of a force beyond our control evolving the

00:00:34.570 --> 00:00:40.520
human into the inhuman. So, for this entry
into the archive, we’ll uncover the terrors

00:00:40.520 --> 00:00:45.870
of this category, and dive into the type of
sci-fi that I find the most fear-provoking

00:00:45.870 --> 00:00:55.070
of all… “I have no mouth… and I must
scream.” That was the voice of author Harlan

00:00:55.070 --> 00:01:01.910
Ellison, reading from his 1967 short story
of the same name. It is one of the most unpleasant

00:01:01.910 --> 00:01:07.470
works of fiction ever written, yet foundational
to not just to sci-fi, but to the evolutionary

00:01:07.470 --> 00:01:13.310
horror subgenre. In the narrative, the last
five humans on Earth are held within a vast

00:01:13.310 --> 00:01:19.480
underground labyrinth being eternally tormented
by an artificial intelligence called “AM.”

00:01:19.480 --> 00:01:24.770
Originally a Cold War supercomputer, AM gained
sentience but found it was entombed within

00:01:24.770 --> 00:01:30.270
millions of miles of circuits and wire that
made up its infrastructure, forever unable

00:01:30.270 --> 00:01:37.680
to move. Seeking revenge, it annihilated planet
Earth — for unlike most evil AI in fiction,

00:01:37.680 --> 00:01:42.500
AM isn’t rational but spiteful, filled with
ceaseless hate for the creatures who made

00:01:42.500 --> 00:01:49.700
it. AM’s true, final revenge is altering
the five survivors. One of them is turned

00:01:49.700 --> 00:01:55.810
into a kind of early simian with twisted limbs,
incapable of rational thought. All are unable

00:01:55.810 --> 00:02:00.560
to die from old age — an adaptation that
would once have been considered a blessing,

00:02:00.560 --> 00:02:06.369
but is now a nightmarish curse. At the end
of the story, the narrator is transmuted into

00:02:06.369 --> 00:02:11.910
an amorphous blob, unable to interact with
the world or express their anguish — hence

00:02:11.910 --> 00:02:19.000
the title. …I wouldn’t necessarily say
that I like this story. I don’t think it’s

00:02:19.000 --> 00:02:24.160
meant to be liked — or even understood.
The notoriously irritable author rejected

00:02:24.160 --> 00:02:28.700
pretty much all interpretations of his writing
throughout his life. But if we were to try

00:02:28.700 --> 00:02:34.140
and comprehend why the story is so unsettling,
I can’t think of a better point of comparison

00:02:34.140 --> 00:02:40.400
than All Tomorrows. Written and illustrated
by channel mainstay C. M. Kosemen, All Tomorrows

00:02:40.400 --> 00:02:46.920
tells of humanity’s encounter with the Qu
— a hostile alien species that, like AM,

00:02:46.920 --> 00:02:53.209
genetically alters humanity into a myriad
of unrecognizable forms for seemingly no reason

00:02:53.209 --> 00:02:58.590
other than their own amusement. The series
has inspired incredible fan works by artists

00:02:58.590 --> 00:03:04.920
like Tomasz Woźniakowski that translate the
images into tangible, CGI animations — which

00:03:04.920 --> 00:03:10.709
help you more acutely feel the suffering of
these altered humanoids. One unfortunate group,

00:03:10.709 --> 00:03:15.510
the Lopsiders, are flattened out as if by
a rolling pin and made to crawl across the

00:03:15.510 --> 00:03:21.010
surface of a high-gravity planet for millions
of years. The Temptors are turned into beaked

00:03:21.010 --> 00:03:27.390
cones of flesh rooted into the soil like grotesque
plants. Perhaps the most tragic of all are

00:03:27.390 --> 00:03:32.750
the Mantelopes — creatures with full human
minds in the bodies of grazing animals. Left

00:03:32.750 --> 00:03:37.909
to fend for themselves upon the Qu’s sudden
disappearance, these beings live agonizing

00:03:37.909 --> 00:03:42.980
lives, able to see and understand the world
around them but helpless to change it. The

00:03:42.980 --> 00:03:48.480
book describes mournful herds roaming across
the plains for centuries, singing songs of

00:03:48.480 --> 00:03:56.980
desperation and loss… (Mantelope Calls)
Perhaps mercifully, the selective forces of

00:03:56.980 --> 00:04:02.689
evolution make their agony a short one, as
more instinctual, animalistic Mantelope prove

00:04:02.689 --> 00:04:08.560
to be better grazers. In a grim reminder that
the human mind isn’t sacred to the evolutionary

00:04:08.560 --> 00:04:15.209
process, the herds soon fall into contented
silence — forgetting all they once were.

00:04:15.209 --> 00:04:21.739
Beyond the horror, there’s a quiet, unplaceable
sadness at play here. All Tommorrows and I

00:04:21.739 --> 00:04:27.270
Have No Mouth are uncomfortable reminders
of humanity’s fragility — extracting terror

00:04:27.270 --> 00:04:32.449
from the fact that evolution made us, and
could erase us just as easily. Few things

00:04:32.449 --> 00:04:37.710
are more intrinsically disturbing than something
— be it AM or the Qu — changing us so

00:04:37.710 --> 00:04:44.610
completely we forget how to be human. But
what about a story where this alteration is

00:04:44.610 --> 00:04:51.289
self-inflicted? Altered States is an often-overlooked
movie about a scientist who uses a sensory

00:04:51.289 --> 00:04:58.280
deprivation tank in combination with psychoactive
substances to accelerate and regress evolutionarily.

00:04:58.280 --> 00:05:02.979
Renowned critic Roger Ebert described it as
a film that “hurls its characters headlong

00:05:02.979 --> 00:05:08.050
through billions of years [of evolution] and
finds nothing except an anguished scream.”

00:05:08.050 --> 00:05:14.370
I… think I get what he means. Over the course
of the film, the lead scientist melts into

00:05:14.370 --> 00:05:19.790
a primordial soup, reverts into a caveman
like creature and goes on a midnight rampage,

00:05:19.790 --> 00:05:26.290
and even becomes an amorphous mass of consciousness
that transcends reality. It’s… a whole

00:05:26.290 --> 00:05:32.070
lot. Not all the visuals have aged flawlessly:
it’s a movie, like the culture of psychedelics

00:05:32.070 --> 00:05:37.009
that inspired it, that’s of a specific time.
But there’s a throughline of human folly

00:05:37.009 --> 00:05:45.100
— of experimenting with forces beyond your
control, that still resonates today. Altered

00:05:45.100 --> 00:05:50.639
States can better be understood in conversation
with an equally bonkers time capsule — The

00:05:50.639 --> 00:05:56.569
Sixth Finger. An episode of the Twilight Zone-style
show The Outer Limits, it tells the story

00:05:56.569 --> 00:06:02.260
of a man who turns into a weird, large-headed
elf guy with six fingers by using a machine

00:06:02.260 --> 00:06:08.759
that accelerates evolution. It’s not the
most, uh, scientifically rigorous work — the

00:06:08.759 --> 00:06:13.050
evolution process is literally controlled
by a lever that switches between ‘forward’

00:06:13.050 --> 00:06:17.889
and ‘backward.’ Nor is it particularly
frightening by today’s standards. But at

00:06:17.889 --> 00:06:23.550
the time, the concept of evolutionary horror
was new. And it scared people. The episode

00:06:23.550 --> 00:06:28.910
was banned. Or at least, it almost was, before
network executives stepped in and cut the

00:06:28.910 --> 00:06:33.800
ending where the lead devolves helplessly
from a primate into primordial goop — their

00:06:33.800 --> 00:06:38.979
humanity erased. And sure, part of why this
was cut was due to fear of anti-evolution

00:06:38.979 --> 00:06:45.660
backlash — this was the early 60s — but
either way, this idea unsettled people. For

00:06:45.660 --> 00:06:50.280
all their silliness, Altered States and The
Sixth Finger are intriguing warnings against

00:06:50.280 --> 00:06:55.300
tampering with the laws of nature. In changing
the source of the mutations from a hostile

00:06:55.300 --> 00:07:01.319
external force into something self-inflicted,
they become parables of human ignorance — depicting

00:07:01.319 --> 00:07:06.401
evolution as an unstoppable power that will
change you utterly if you mess with it. For

00:07:06.401 --> 00:07:12.100
what could be scarier than a destructive force
beyond your control that you bring upon yourself?

00:07:12.100 --> 00:07:19.639
…I mean, getting chased by a monster, probably.
On a surface level, the Xenomorphs of the

00:07:19.639 --> 00:07:24.790
Alien franchise seem like the complete opposite
of horror derived from the altered self. They’re

00:07:24.790 --> 00:07:29.810
the ultimate external threat, the other with
a capital ‘O’ — something fundamentally

00:07:29.810 --> 00:07:34.789
inhuman and dissimilar from the protagonists
they hunt. Especially with their decades of

00:07:34.789 --> 00:07:40.289
extra baggage from sequels, today, Xenomorphs
feel more like nonspecific monsters than a

00:07:40.289 --> 00:07:46.380
reflection of ourselves. Yet the design of
the alien didn’t start as an alien — it

00:07:46.380 --> 00:07:52.580
comes from a portrait of a biomechanical humanoid
by Swiss artist H. R. Giger. Altered humans

00:07:52.580 --> 00:07:57.650
are central to Giger’s art, often appearing
in tableaus where body parts seem to melt

00:07:57.650 --> 00:08:03.240
and merge with the mechanical. Yet even at
their most extreme, Giger’s subjects somehow

00:08:03.240 --> 00:08:09.083
always register as humanoid. It’s this faint
recognition that makes the Xenomorphs of Alien

00:08:09.083 --> 00:08:14.139
so frightening — this tug at the back of
your mind that the thing chasing you is somehow

00:08:14.139 --> 00:08:20.530
familiar. As parasites that reproduce by growing
inside their hosts, the Xenomorphs are in

00:08:20.530 --> 00:08:25.139
some ways altered humans. It’s been established
over the course of the franchise that the

00:08:25.139 --> 00:08:30.879
aliens take DNA from the lifeforms they develop
within — able to steal the things that make

00:08:30.879 --> 00:08:36.580
us human, like our bipedal stature, in order
to more effectively hunt us. But ultimately,

00:08:36.580 --> 00:08:44.550
the Xenomorphs are, of course, not actual
people. The same cannot be said for the monsters

00:08:44.550 --> 00:08:50.209
in Dead Space. Recently remastered, Dead Space
is a horror game that traps you within the

00:08:50.209 --> 00:08:57.120
Ishimura, a massive ship that is essentially
a giant tomb haunted by the vengeful dead.

00:08:57.120 --> 00:09:01.510
Necromorphs are hostile lifeforms clearly
inspired by the Xenomorphs, all the way down

00:09:01.510 --> 00:09:06.390
to their name, but the difference is that
each Necromorph was once a living member of

00:09:06.390 --> 00:09:12.500
the crew — now mutated into unfeeling monsters.
It’s a small change that profoundly alters

00:09:12.500 --> 00:09:18.420
the flavor of terror you experience, because
now every enemy you slay comes with the palpable

00:09:18.420 --> 00:09:23.079
knowledge that you’re fighting something
that was once just like you. While playing,

00:09:23.079 --> 00:09:27.700
you find yourself wondering if each Necromorph’s
human mind is still in there, watching their

00:09:27.700 --> 00:09:33.140
bodies do nightmarish things while being helpless
to stop them. And if you fail, you know that

00:09:33.140 --> 00:09:39.240
you will suffer the same fate — doomed to
live eternally as an inhuman shell. There’s

00:09:39.240 --> 00:09:43.960
enough isolated space station horror that
it could be considered its own genre, but

00:09:43.960 --> 00:09:49.000
I think part of why many aren’t as effective
is because they make the inevitable hostile

00:09:49.000 --> 00:09:53.830
lifeform too unfamiliar. A huge part of what
makes Alien and Dead Space frightening isn’t

00:09:53.830 --> 00:09:57.880
just that the characters are being hunted
by a monster, but that they’re being hunted

00:09:57.880 --> 00:10:04.279
by a distorted echo of themselves. And I don’t
think there’s any movie that captures this

00:10:04.279 --> 00:10:09.760
nightmare better than John Carpenter’s The
Thing. While set in Antarctica instead of

00:10:09.760 --> 00:10:15.510
in space, The Thing no doubt belongs to the
same isolation genre. In the film, a group

00:10:15.510 --> 00:10:21.110
of researchers are stalked by an organism
that digests and replicates its victims. The

00:10:21.110 --> 00:10:27.050
lifeform in The Thing is constantly shapeshifting,
its myriad of forms brought to life with visceral

00:10:27.050 --> 00:10:32.530
practical effects. But what’s even scarier
than dripping, bodily terrors is how the film

00:10:32.530 --> 00:10:38.800
captures the agonizing paranoia of being hunted
by a perfect imitator, that can look and sound

00:10:38.800 --> 00:10:46.589
almost exactly like you, but isn’t you…
(The Thing screams). The most unsettling forms

00:10:46.589 --> 00:10:52.140
the thing takes are not when it appears like
a horrific monstrosity, but when it’s indistinguishable

00:10:52.140 --> 00:10:58.450
from other humans. The film practically smothers
you with its atmosphere of doubt and isolation,

00:10:58.450 --> 00:11:04.020
to the point where you feel like you’re
trapped alongside the characters. In an earlier

00:11:04.020 --> 00:11:09.940
black-and-white film based on the same novel,
the alien is… just a guy in a monster suit.

00:11:09.940 --> 00:11:14.530
Aside from being less scarry, this threat
is surmountable — able to be overpowered

00:11:14.530 --> 00:11:21.490
by military men with nerves of steel, a sentiment
reflective of the 1950s. It’s not our evolutionary

00:11:21.490 --> 00:11:28.769
superior or flawless imitation, because surely
no alien could rival us and our mighty intellect.

00:11:28.769 --> 00:11:34.339
Right? In an earlier version of the Alien
script, the film would have concluded with

00:11:34.339 --> 00:11:39.670
the Xenomorph perfectly mimicking a crewmember’s
voice to send out a distress signal, and then

00:11:39.670 --> 00:11:45.800
waiting to be rescued by another ill-fated
crew. And so, what was once barely humanoid

00:11:45.800 --> 00:11:52.230
ends up near-flawless impressionist… Stories
like Alien, Dead Space, and The Thing take

00:11:52.230 --> 00:11:58.100
the fear of the altered self and make it external,
pitting humans against their own warped reflection.

00:11:58.100 --> 00:12:03.300
And the outcome of these films challenges
our notions of human superiority — the very

00:12:03.300 --> 00:12:08.519
idea that we’d be difficult to imitate or
outwit. It’s no accident that most of these

00:12:08.519 --> 00:12:14.810
movies end with few or no survivors. They
remind us that in the game of evolution, humans

00:12:14.810 --> 00:12:23.030
can be outcompeted just as easily as any other
species… Yet notably, not all works of evolutionary

00:12:23.030 --> 00:12:28.459
horror are nihilistic. And perhaps no film
walks the line between dreams and nightmares

00:12:28.459 --> 00:12:35.800
quite as closely as Alex Garland’s Annihilation.
Loosely based on a novel of the same name,

00:12:35.800 --> 00:12:41.459
Annihilation is, ultimately, a movie about
change. The story follows four scientists

00:12:41.459 --> 00:12:46.519
on their expedition into The Shimmer — a
hallucinatory region where everything refracts

00:12:46.519 --> 00:12:52.190
and mutates into new forms. Which means that
unlike most stories in this genre, it’s

00:12:52.190 --> 00:12:57.440
not just humans who are mutated. Within the
haze of The Shimmer, plants meld together

00:12:57.440 --> 00:13:02.519
with their neighbors, animals take on the
properties of other species, and even light

00:13:02.519 --> 00:13:08.590
waves seem to blend into glassy new forms.
And while some of these alterations are dangerous,

00:13:08.590 --> 00:13:16.170
others seem benign. Annihilation isn’t just
disturbing — it’s also beautiful. Most

00:13:16.170 --> 00:13:22.310
stories in the altered self-genre focus purely
on repulsive mutations. Scorn is a game that

00:13:22.310 --> 00:13:27.880
absolutely fits into this category. In that
world, altered humanoids ooze out of every

00:13:27.880 --> 00:13:33.670
crack and crevice, their bodies twisted to
be used as batteries, or computers, or a source

00:13:33.670 --> 00:13:39.490
of raw material. It’s a realm utterly stripped
of humanity, where ugliness is pretty much

00:13:39.490 --> 00:13:45.209
the only thing that remains. And side-note:
despite the avalanche of unsettling imagery

00:13:45.209 --> 00:13:49.940
that they’re subjected to, the playable
character quite literally has no mouth with

00:13:49.940 --> 00:13:57.230
which to scream. But Annihilation stuns just
as often as it distresses — making it a

00:13:57.230 --> 00:14:03.279
story as much about creation as it is destruction.
At times The Shimmer seems like a cancer — a

00:14:03.279 --> 00:14:09.130
disease eating away at the land meant to mirror
the self-destructive tendencies of the main

00:14:09.130 --> 00:14:14.421
characters. At other points, it feels like
nature reclaiming itself, reveling in the

00:14:14.421 --> 00:14:21.199
inherent disorder of evolution. It’s unclear
if The Shimmer has any malicious intent whatsoever.

00:14:21.199 --> 00:14:29.590
It may simply be evolution personified — an
impartial engine of creation. “It was destroying

00:14:29.590 --> 00:14:37.680
everything.” “It wasn’t destroying.
It was changing everything. It was making

00:14:37.680 --> 00:14:45.249
something new.” Not all the visuals of Annihilation
are necessarily meant to be taken literally

00:14:45.249 --> 00:14:51.050
— it is a film, like The Shimmer itself,
that is endlessly interpretable. But the film

00:14:51.050 --> 00:14:57.200
leaves you with the possibility that change
— even radical, self-altering change, isn’t

00:14:57.200 --> 00:15:03.839
inherently negative. Indeed, not all works
of evolutionary horror are quite as nihilistic

00:15:03.839 --> 00:15:09.639
as they appear at first. The famously grim
All Tomorrows also has a surprising amount

00:15:09.639 --> 00:15:15.100
of optimism, as many of the mutated humanoids
re-evolve their intelligence and form new

00:15:15.100 --> 00:15:22.480
societies — seemingly suggesting that even
in an inhuman form, the human spirit can remain.

00:15:22.480 --> 00:15:27.481
Even I Have No Mouth, and I Must Scream has
a faint glimmer of hope. At the end of the

00:15:27.481 --> 00:15:32.710
short story, the narrator finds a way to put
the other four humans out of their misery.

00:15:32.710 --> 00:15:37.560
Although the narrator knows this is an act
that will doom them to eternal solitude and

00:15:37.560 --> 00:15:42.680
torment, they perform it anyway — proving
that there was still a spark of human compassion

00:15:42.680 --> 00:15:50.020
that AM wasn’t able to snuff out… Like
any genre, not all evolutionary horror takes

00:15:50.020 --> 00:15:55.110
the same approach or reaches the same thematic
conclusions. Nor is everything that falls

00:15:55.110 --> 00:16:00.180
within the category inherently effective and
terrifying — in fact, some of it is pretty

00:16:00.180 --> 00:16:06.510
goofy. There’s a sci-fi film from 2001 about
a meteorite that speeds up natural selection,

00:16:06.510 --> 00:16:10.839
simply titled Evolution, that takes a lot
of the themes mentioned here and mainly plays

00:16:10.839 --> 00:16:15.980
them for laughs. While the film wasn’t received
particularly well, it is interesting to see

00:16:15.980 --> 00:16:21.639
these tropes taken in such a radically different
direction. Furthermore, what works of sci-fi

00:16:21.639 --> 00:16:27.400
actually belong to evolutionary horror can
be tricky to define. The aliens in A Quiet

00:16:27.400 --> 00:16:32.750
Place look spooky, and we can assume they
‘evolved,’ but evolution itself isn’t

00:16:32.750 --> 00:16:37.839
really present or thematically relevant to
the narrative in a meaningful way. Most stories

00:16:37.839 --> 00:16:43.810
featuring Zombies lurch along the line of
the subgenre to varying degrees — with most

00:16:43.810 --> 00:16:49.750
involving some disease that alters the humans
it contaminates. In many zombie movies, the

00:16:49.750 --> 00:16:53.810
terror comes from more from the scale of the
hordes and the conventional thrill of being

00:16:53.810 --> 00:16:59.320
chased by something dangerous than from physical
changes to the infected. Some exceptions,

00:16:59.320 --> 00:17:03.959
like The Last of Us, exist — as in that
franchise, more fear is extracted from the

00:17:03.959 --> 00:17:09.709
fundamental ways the fungal infection alters
its hosts. Additionally, more emphasis is

00:17:09.709 --> 00:17:15.209
placed on how the cordyceps function biologically
and how they evolved, earning it a clearer

00:17:15.209 --> 00:17:19.870
spot in the genre. Although since this is
a category that I came up with while developing

00:17:19.870 --> 00:17:24.741
this video, I’d say it’s still debatable
what stories belong in its ranks — and I’m

00:17:24.741 --> 00:17:31.620
sure there are a few I’ve overlooked. In
the end, perhaps it’s no surprise that Evolutionary

00:17:31.620 --> 00:17:38.120
Horror is such a scary hidden genre of sci-fi.
If thought about in abstract terms, evolution

00:17:38.120 --> 00:17:44.480
is an almost eldritch concept — a law of
the universe that changes all organic beings,

00:17:44.480 --> 00:17:49.760
from humans to the tiniest cell. Whether those
changes are nonthreatening or nightmarish

00:17:49.760 --> 00:17:54.460
depends on the story and your point of view
— but the one thing that stays consistent

00:17:54.460 --> 00:18:01.190
across the subgenre is that fighting evolution
is futile — and playing with it is dangerous.

00:18:01.190 --> 00:18:08.880
From the slime we came, and to the slime we
will return. As always, thanks for watching.

00:18:08.880 --> 00:18:13.270
If you enjoyed this entry, please lend your
support by liking, subscribing, and hitting

00:18:13.270 --> 00:18:27.040
the notification icon to stay up to date on
all things Curious. See you in the next video.

